  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThe proposed approach combines a hierarchical optimization strategy with randomized rounding and function interpolation. Unlike previous methods, it first optimizes the function representation in low-dimensional grids to reduce computational complexity, then employs a coarse-to-fine interpolation strategy to maintain solution quality. Key improvements include:\n   - Using grid hierarchy from small resolutions (2, 4...) to full size, preserving optimal features\n   - Adaptive projection to desired grid size\n   - Randomized rounding during grid transition\n   - Progressive temperature annealing in continuous-to-binary transitions\n\nPotential bottleneck: Grid interpolation must preserve important function features. We address this by orientation-preserving interpolation and domain-aware repair.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n   - Decision variables: h(x) defined on [0,2] with \u222bh(x)dx=1\n   - Objective: min max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n   - Constraints: 0\u2264h(x)\u22641, \u222bh(x)dx=1\n\nProjected gradient descent: \n   - Smooth \u21132 penalty for equality constraint\n   - Bregman projection for box constraints\n   - Specified optimization steps using COBYLA solver\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   3.1 Parent Validation\n      - Check input parent is non-empty and has odd n\n      - Verify sum(h) \u2248 n/2 (clipped to integer)\n      - Enforce domain [0,1] and integral constraint after all operations\n\n   3.2 Coarse-Grained Initialization\n      - Start with n_min=2 parents (sequences of 0/1 with sum n_min/2)\n      - Select n_min parents with most selective features\n      - Compute C5 scores for each using mode=\"full\"\n\n   3.3 Progressive Refinement\n      - Set \u03b3 = 0.05 (frequency of grid resolution changes)\n      - For iteration count t:\n          - With probability 1-\u03b3: Function interpolation (orientation-preserving)\n          - With probability \u03b3: Grid resolution change (up/down by factor 2)\n        Algorithm for refinement:\n          function adaptive_refinement(h_current, \u03b2, penalty, time_budget):\n            while time_budget > 1.0 and t < t_max:\n              t += 1\n              time_used += time_for_component\n              apply_grid_search(h_current)   [grid hierarchy: 2,4,...,n]\n              apply_representation_change(h_current) [binary to continuous or vice versa]\n              compute_obj_and_constr(h)   [enforce C=0.38]\n              use project_box_sum for repair\n\n   3.4 Optimizer Interface\n      Inputs:\n        h0: initial grid representation (binary)\n        n_points: target grid size (2 to 606)\n        \u03b2: penalty parameter (100 to 1000)\n        max_iter: \u2264 5000\n        time_limit: 1300s\n      Solver: COBYLA with box constraints [0,1]\n      Objective: max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx * dx\n      Constraints:\n        g1 = max(h) - 1 \u2264 0\n        g2 = min(h) - 0 \u2264 0\n        g3 = sum(h) - n/2 = 0 (enforced as equality constraint with penalty)\n\n   3.5 Representation Change\n      function revolve_orientation(h):\n        if grid_size == 2:\n          perform low-to-high interpolation\n        elif grid_size > 4:\n          perform high-to-low projection\n        else:\n          raise error\n\n      function orientation_preserving_grid_interp(h_src, n_dst):\n        n_orig = len(h_src)\n        logn_orig = math.log2(n_orig)\n        logn_dst = math.log2(n_dst)\n        # Preserve function orientation using tensor product\n        if n_dst > n_orig:\n          raise SizeError(\"Destination larger than source\")\n        elif n_dst < n_orig:\n          # Use specialized B-spline projector\n          design_matrix = b_spline_basis(n_orig, n_dst)\n          h_dst = np.linalg.solve(design_matrix.T @ design_matrix + 1e-5*np.eye(rnk), \n                                  design_matrix.T * some_weights)\n          return np.squeeze(h_dst)\n        else:\n          return np.copy(h_dst)\n\n   3.6 Adaptive Scheduling\n      - Re-sampling adjustment: Maintain history of best/second-best solutions\n      - Progressive cooling: T(t) = T0 * (1-1e-3t_max)^(t/t_max)\n      - Hierarchical grid selection: Prioritize grids around 100-200 points\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n   4.1 Core Strategies:\n      - Primary: Function interpolation + grid resolution maintenance\n      - Secondary: Orthonormal basis projection, randomized rounding\n      - Backup: Feature-preservation during grid transitions\n\n   4.2 Parameter Variations:\n      Typical \u03b2: 100, variation: [2*\u03b2_min, \u03b2/2]\n      Typical n_min: 2, variation: [2, 8]\n      Typical time for grid change: 80% reduction of regular steps\n\n   5. Computational Budget\n      - Estimated dominant cost: 30% in grid hierarchy processing\n      - Cooling schedule: Decrease every 500 steps (cooling factor 0.95)\n      - Grid size changes occur once every 10 steps\n      - Monitoring check every 25 steps for \u2308dt/dt_max\u2309 utility\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n   Final checks:\n     def validate_solution(h, n, budget_remaining):\n         assert n>=2 and n even, \"Grid size must be even\"\n         assert min(h)>=0 and max(h)<=1, \"Values not within bounds\"\n         assert abs(sum(h) - n/2) <= 1e-2, \"Sum constraint violated\"\n         if budget_remaining < 30:\n             enforce feasibility via project_box_sum(h, n)\n         return h, compute_c5(h, mode=\"full\")",
